items.statement:
Let a closed four-vertex network have edges $(1,2)$ and $(3,4)$ with
conductances $1$ and $3$. Its steady stiffness is singular: each disconnected
positive-conductance component contributes an independent constant kernel
mode. The load $(1,0,0,0)^{\mathsf T}$ is incompatible with the first
component's Neumann balance, so it has no steady solution.

The two disconnected edges and their conductances are shown below; all vertex masses are one, and the right-hand side is $b=(1,0,0,0)^{\mathsf T}$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\small}]
  \node[circle,draw] (v1) at (0,0) {$1$};
  \node[circle,draw] (v2) at (1.7,0) {$2$};
  \node[circle,draw] (v3) at (3.5,0) {$3$};
  \node[circle,draw] (v4) at (5.2,0) {$4$};
  \draw[->] (v1) -- node[above] {$c=1$} (v2);
  \draw[->] (v3) -- node[above] {$c=3$} (v4);
  \node[below=4pt] at (v1) {$b_1=1$};
  \node[below=4pt] at (v2) {$b_2=0$};
  \node[below=4pt] at (v3) {$b_3=0$};
  \node[below=4pt] at (v4) {$b_4=0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

Take three unit edges oriented from a central vertex $v$ to leaves $a,b,c$,
with unit diffusivity and prescribed leaf values $u(a)=2$, $u(b)=0$, and
$u(c)=1$. Define on the three edge coordinates
$u_1(x)=1+x$, $u_2(x)=1-x$, and $u_3(x)=1$ for $0\le x\le1$. The common
central trace is $1$, and the total outward diffusive flux at $v$ is zero.

The arrows give the coordinate orientations from the central vertex; the drawing is schematic and the proof uses the exact endpoint values below.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, every node/.style={font=\small}]
  \node[circle,draw] (v) at (0,0) {$v:1$};
  \node[circle,draw] (a) at (-2,1.4) {$a:2$};
  \node[circle,draw] (b) at (2,1.4) {$b:0$};
  \node[circle,draw] (c) at (0,-2) {$c:1$};
  \draw[->] (v) -- node[above left] {$u_1(x)=1+x$} (a);
  \draw[->] (v) -- node[above right] {$u_2(x)=1-x$} (b);
  \draw[->] (v) -- node[right] {$u_3(x)=1$} (c);
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

Consider a closed three-vertex path with orientation $1\to2\to3$, masses
$(1,2,3)$, conductances $(1,2)$, homogeneous diffusion, and initial state
$u(0)=(2,1,4)^{\mathsf T}$. Its conserved weighted mass is $16$. The
constant state $(8/3,8/3,8/3)^{\mathsf T}$ is an equilibrium with that same
weighted mass, and the exact trajectory from the stated nonnegative initial
state stays entrywise nonnegative. No convergence-to-equilibrium claim is made.

The diagram records the orientation, conductances, and vertex masses; edge lengths are schematic and are not used in the matrix calculation.

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, every node/.style={font=\small}]
  \node[circle,draw] (v1) at (0,0) {$1$};
  \node[circle,draw] (v2) at (2.1,0) {$2$};
  \node[circle,draw] (v3) at (4.2,0) {$3$};
  \draw[->] (v1) -- node[above] {$c_1=1$} (v2);
  \draw[->] (v2) -- node[above] {$c_2=2$} (v3);
  \node[below=4pt] at (v1) {$m_1=1$};
  \node[below=4pt] at (v2) {$m_2=2$};
  \node[below=4pt] at (v3) {$m_3=3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}
